  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nNo significant changes from the previous forecast package. Mid-level \nremnants of what was once Edouard will shift southwestward over \nSouth Texas today and move into Mexico late tonight, ushering in \nmoisture well above-normal (PWATs 2-2.25\"). The Victoria Crossroads \nwill be the most favorable area for convection in South Texas, with \nmore moisture, instability, and where mid-level PVA maxima resides. \nTherefore a 20-40% chance of showers and thunderstorms remains in \nthe forecast for the Victoria Crossroads today, with everywhere else \nwest very low at a 10-20%. I wouldn't be surprised for a few showers \nand thunderstorms to develop off the seabreeze given the abundance \nof moisture and what occurred yesterday afternoon. Rain chances \nincrease a tad this Labor Day Weekend to 30-50%, with lingering \nchances into early-mid next week as additional tropical waves \ncontinue to usher in moisture and feed seabreeze convection.\n\nAs mentioned previously, temperatures will be difficult and least \nconfident over the Victoria Crossroads where greater rain and sky \ncoverage may be in play. According to the latest HREF probabilities, \nmax temperatures are now only about at a 20% chance to go below 95 \ndegrees over Victoria. The additional sky cover and rain coverage \nshould keep us just below Heat Advisory criteria through the \nweekend. However, the WBGT UIL risk will remain in the moderate risk \nlevel with heat indices 105-110 and corresponding air temperatures \nfrom the mid 90s along the coast to 100-105 over the Brush\nCountry.",
